# Go project layout

Source of truth: [Organizing a Go module](https://go.dev/doc/modules/layout),
[Module version numbering](https://go.dev/doc/modules/version-numbers).
These are the layouts the Go team documents. `pkg/`, `api/`, `configs/` and the rest
of the widely copied "standard project layout" are **not** part of them — do not
introduce those directories unless the repository already uses them.

## Pick the layout that matches the project type

| Project type | Layout |
| --- | --- |
| Basic package | `go.mod` + `modname.go` + `modname_test.go` at the root |
| Basic command | `go.mod` + `main.go` (and friends) at the root, all `package main` |
| Package with supporting code | Root package + `internal/<name>/` for everything not exported |
| Multiple packages | One directory per package, hierarchical, plus `internal/` |
| Multiple commands | `cmd/prog1/main.go`, `cmd/prog2/main.go`, shared code in `internal/` |
| Server | `cmd/<binary>/main.go` for each binary, **all logic in `internal/`** |

## Rules that follow from that

- Default to `internal/`. Code under `internal/` cannot be imported by other modules,
  so you stay free to refactor it. Export a package only when an external caller
  genuinely needs it and you are prepared to support it forever.
- A server is a self-contained binary: it should normally export nothing. If part of
  it becomes useful elsewhere, split that part into its own module.
- The `cmd/` directory is only needed when the repository mixes commands with
  importable packages, or has more than one binary. A single-binary repo can put
  `main.go` at the root.
- The `module` line must be the repository URL without a scheme
  (`module github.com/someuser/modname`), because that is how the `go` command
  locates the source.
- Files in a directory all declare the same package. Test files are `_test.go`
  in the same directory.

## Versioning

- Semantic versioning is a contract, not decoration:
  - `v0.x.x` — unstable, no compatibility guarantee.
  - `vX.Y.**Z**` — no public API or dependency change.
  - `vX.**Y**.x` — backward-compatible additions.
  - `**vX**.y.z` — backward-incompatible changes.
- Major versions `v2` and above change the module path:
  `module example.com/mymodule/v2`. Imports change too. Treat a major bump as the
  last resort, not the routine way to ship a breaking change.
- Never hand-write a pseudo-version (`v0.0.0-20170915032832-14c0d48ead0c`).
  Let the `go` command generate it.
- The `go` line in `go.mod` is a hard minimum: a toolchain refuses to build a module
  that requires a newer version than itself, and your `go` line must be at least as
  new as every module you require.
